ements, but it feels premature since this will clearly be needed for future claim logic. I think it's better to leave it than to remove and re-add it later.\n\nI'm also noticing the `contractIndex` mutation inside the map function—it works fine but feels a bit awkward. The key insight is that only quote steps increment it, so claim steps don't advance the contract count, which is important behavior to preserve. Let me look for a cleaner approach elsewhere. One thing that stands out is using `stepIndex` as the policy key, which happens to work in the test but feels coincidental—the policy reference should probably be more explicit than just relying on step ordering.\n\nActually, the cleanest small improvement would be extracting the claim handling into its own function, symmetric with quote handling. And I could simplify the `contractFactor` logic by replacing the magic `contractIndex === 0` check with an `isFirstContract` boolean parameter—that's clearer about intent.\n\nLooking at the tradeoff: passing `contractFactor(contractIndex === 0)` moves the conditional to the caller, which doesn't really save complexity. Another option is using `policies.size` to track prior contracts instead of maintaining a separate counter, but that couples contract numbering to policy count and loses clarity about what the number represents.\n\nI think the cleaner move is extracting helper functions like `handleQuote` and `handleClaim` within `runScenario` to make the map body more readable. Or I could do a smaller refactor—maybe renaming `insuranceSum` for clarity or extracting the quote handling logic to its own function.\n\nActually, the real win here is addressing that magic number `2` in the insurance cap calculation. I'll introduce a `INSURANCE_CAP_MULTIPLIER` constant and create a helper function `policyCap(items)` that documents the intent behind doubling the insurance sum. The `payout: 0` during the green phase can stay as-is since the red phase will change it anyway.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll examine the code and identify a refactoring opportunity.\n\n## Naming Evaluation\n\n- `runScenario`, `quote`, `basePremiumForItems`, `itemMultiplier`, `contractFactor`, `loyaltyFactor` — all clear and reveal intent.\n- `insuranceSum` — clear.\n- **Issue found**: The expression `2 * insuranceSum(step.items)` in `runScenario` uses a magic number `2` with no explanation. This is the \"insurance cap\" (2× insurance sum) — a distinct domain concept. It hides intent (Rule 2).\n\n## Simple Design Evaluation\n\n- **Rule 1 (tests pass)**: ✅ 14/14 passing\n- **Rule 2 (reveals intent)**: The `2 * insuranceSum(...)` inline computation obscures that this is the policy's total payout cap. Extracting a named helper will make the claim-related code (which is about to grow) clearer.\n- **Rule 3 (no duplication)**: Only one use today, but the concept (cap = 2× insurance sum) is a first-class domain rule that will recur as claim tests cap-track it.\n- **Rule 4 (fewest elements)**: Adding one small helper; caller becomes clearer.\n\n## APP Mass (relevant slice — the `runScenario` map body)\n\n**Before:**\n```ts\npolicies.set(stepIndex, { items: step.items, remainingCap: 2 * insuranceSum(step.items) });\n```\n- Constants: 1 (`2`) = 1\n- Bindings: 4 (`policies`, `stepIndex`, `step.items` ×2 → treat as 2 accesses) = 4\n- Invocations: 3 (`policies.set`, `insuranceSum`, `*`) = 6\n- Assignments: 0\n- **Slice mass: 11**\n\n**After:**\n```ts\npolicies.set(stepIndex, { items: step.items, remainingCap: policyCapFor(step.items) });\n```\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: 3 = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (`policies.set`, `policyCapFor`) = 4\n- **Slice mass: 7** (delta −4)\n\nPlus the new helper `policyCapFor` adds a small fixed mass, but it names the domain concept — Rule 2 wins over raw mass either way."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01CS6n1A1JyQzrcmB7REN89g","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const in